WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.169 --> 00:00:03.010
There exists in Dota a prestigious award.

00:00:03.010 --> 00:00:06.440
A digital medal handed out to only the most heroic players.

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:09.340
The players who sacrifice themselves for the team.

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:12.200
The players who persevere through every hardship.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:16.560
The players who feed incessantly and still make it out the other side.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:22.160
That award is the Maelk award, and it is given only to players who do incredible things

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:25.240
in the face of unbelievable KDAs.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:35.580
The Maelk award is somehow both an honor and a dig on the player that earns it.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:42.360
It represents teamwork, sacrifice, perseverance and perhaps most importantly, incessant feeding.

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:48.780
It requires one player to go through a game with zero kills and at least 20 deaths, and then still somehow win.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:52.120
It seems impossible, but this man did it.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:58.860
“It’s fun, it’s great, they had their fun, we won the game, everyone wins, the meme

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:01.020
was created, the community wins.”

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:05.480
The Maelk award was born when this man, Jacob “Maelk” Toft-Anderson was playing on EG

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:07.400
in the ancient days of 2012.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:11.200
Maelk was playing Position 4 for EG, and the team was still trying to get

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:12.700
their feet on the ground.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:24.320
(Casting)

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:29.680
But it was the early days of Dota 2, and

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:35.100
it was a total wild west, and that meant that teams like the 2012 Mousesports roster could thrive.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:39.440
"Hi, so how are you spending your time in Seattle so far if you're not gaming."

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:41.160
"Masturbating."

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:42.240
"Sorry?"

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:43.100
"Masturbating."

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:49.080
"So essentially we're playing this meme team from Mousesports, they have all these dumbasses on their

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:49.700
roster."

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:51.780
"How hard was that game?"

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:55.460
"It was probably close to my easiest game of my life."

00:01:55.460 --> 00:02:01.840
"They have SingSing, they have Trixi, they have all these morons who don't know how to win in Dota."

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:06.660
"They know how to have fun though, and I respect that because gaming is ultimately about having fun

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:07.780
first and foremost."

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:10.640
"Alright thank you very much SingSing for coming on the show."

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:14.320
"Good luck in the future games and we'll see you later."

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:17.060
"Thank you thank you, I'm gonna go mastur-"

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:21.460
"F***ing SingSing"

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:24.960
But if mouz were memeing in the online joinDOTA Masters league, EG

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:29.520
were taking things seriously, they found themselves ahead in gold, but behind in XP in the

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.360
early game thanks to a few early kills from mouz.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:46.380
(Casting)

00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:47.960
And after scoring a few early

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:52.200
kills on Maelk’s Venomancer they thought it’d be hilarious to just keep killing him.

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:53.640
So they did.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:56.280
(Casting)

00:02:56.380 --> 00:02:57.080
Over...

00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:03.200
(Casting)

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:04.720
And over...

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:11.840
(Casting)

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:12.800
And over...

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:18.860
(Casting)

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:20.000
And over...

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:34.920
(Casting)

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:36.900
While EG was farming well and maintained

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:40.320
a gold lead for most of the game, mouz still had the XP advantage,

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.480
and it was starting to matter a lot more.

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:53.000
(Casting)

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:57.840
By the end of the game, Maelk had died 20 times without picking up a single kill, but

00:03:57.840 --> 00:03:59.140
it wasn’t for lack of trying.

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:02.740
While mouz decided it would be funny to keep killing Maelk, he was trying

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:04.140
to help his team win.

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:08.260
And even though he kept dying, sometimes he'd get the chance to pop his ultimate first.

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:11.740
Venomancer’s Poison Nova ability can’t actually kill anything.

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:15.879
It creates a ring of poison that spreads out and deals damage over time to every enemy

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:18.100
in it, but it leaves them at 1 HP.

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:22.260
That means even though Maelk dealt tons of damage to mouz, he never actually got any

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:23.440
kill credit for it.

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:25.360
But the rest of his team did.

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:39.820
(Casting)

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:41.460
It didn’t matter much though.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:43.460
Both teams were kind of a mess.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:49.060
EG were effectively playing with only four heroes, and while mouz were winning fights, they weren’t

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:50.800
winning hard enough to end the game.

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:54.500
"My first 10 deaths, some of them should have been avoided by me,

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:58.980
some of them was just good play by them. When we go into the later stages of the game,

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:02.540
by all means we should have lost. They had a full inventory Morphling,

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:04.400
they were decked out on all their heroes."

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:23.040
(Casting)

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:25.880
"They by all means should have won with the lineup they had."

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:32.300
"What they did though, they realized, 'Oh this guy has so many deaths, let's just see how many we can keep on

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:36.460
adding on to that.' You know I think at some point in the game some of them would write 'xD'

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:38.540
and sit and talk about how funny this was."

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:46.020
(Casting)

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:51.600
And so, after over an hour of play, EG decided it was time to keep forcing themselves

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:53.920
into mouz’s base until it stuck.

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:57.000
But not before Maelk died one more time.

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:03.940
(Casting)

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:09.440
And while Maelk may have died one last time, EG’s creeps finally managed to close out the game

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:11.880
after an hour and 26 minutes.

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:24.780
(Casting)

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:29.240
Maelk finished the game 0/20/21, and EG still managed to win.

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:32.780
That seems impossible but there's a few reasons for why it happened.

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:36.120
For one, mouz was really only focused on killing Maelk.

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:37.960
And another thing was that,

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:41.800
it was still the early days of Dota 2, and people just weren’t as good at playing

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:43.040
the game as they are now.

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:57.480
(Casting)

00:06:57.480 --> 00:06:59.000
But the statline stuck.

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:00.280
It became an award.

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:05.040
The player who manages to go 20 deaths without a single kill and still win the game does

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:06.340
deserve recognition.

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:11.000
It’s mostly applied to amateur games, where players, casters and pros alike have witnessed

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:16.180
heroes who have bravely given themselves over to the Maelk Award’s hallowed halls of esteemed recipients.

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:23.220
"I've had a decent amount of years to sort of get used to it. I very rarely meet people for the first time without them

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:28.920
remembering that particular game, so I'm very used to the fact that people bring it up."

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:35.820
"I've also hosted, well not hosted but I've been on the desk in so many tournaments where my co-analysts keep bringing it up and finding it

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:42.840
amusing. When Paul was new to Dota 2 he also used it as a cornerstone of his mockery."

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:48.620
"And finally, last but not least, our panel wouldn't be complete without a star name. A former pro player

00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:53.160
with expert knowledge and predictions, and a popular figure with the community.

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:55.300
Sadly, we could only get Maelk."

00:07:56.900 --> 00:08:01.760
"I've had to grow some hard skin whenever that was brought up."

00:08:01.780 --> 00:08:05.960
And Maelk himself is totally okay with the fact that his name is attached to what isn’t

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:09.280
exactly the most prestigious award in Dota history.

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:11.540
That’s probably because he won the game.

00:08:11.620 --> 00:08:16.480
(Casting)

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:20.000
And honestly that’s what makes the Maelk award so impressive.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:22.680
Maelk did more in death than he did in life.

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:25.900
And his team found a way to use the fact that he was dying so often

00:08:25.900 --> 00:08:27.240
to their advantage.

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:31.720
Dota is unique among MOBAs in that deaths don’t matter as much as they do in a game like say,

00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:32.640
League of Legends.

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:44.160
so you can actually make it back despite having a player on your team who has more

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:45.520
deaths than levels.

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:49.420
Dota is a game of teamwork, and that means that even if someone looks like they're feeding,

00:08:49.420 --> 00:08:53.080
it might just be an elaborate plan concocted by their team to win.

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:56.060
Or they might be getting camped by some trolls.

00:08:56.060 --> 00:08:57.520
Either way, they'll get an award for it.

